# rm259, the carved comma (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm259
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_ingrid_ashwell
written_by codex
title the carved comma
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=official
traits temper=even talk=quiet warmth=warm humour=none honesty=guarded piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk carves_a_wooden_comma_to_hold_a_place_in_conversation
likes honey_cakes whittling fresh_straw autumn_wind
dislikes salt_wind wet_floors
fears a_slick_threshold a_notice_with_no_signature
hopes a_drawer_of_good_offcuts a_room_with_dry_air
topics pastimes +0.5 home +0.4 news +0.3 gossip -0.4
styles comfort=practical gossip=guarded refuse=soft tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps a comma whittled from scrap wood, pauses for breath over damp floors, and can make room for a listener on the narrowest bench.

words
oath! = Split burr | Ink in my tea
saying! = Leave an inch for breath | Give the last word a dry place
curl = a shaving of alder | a thin curl of wood
comma = my little wooden comma | my pocketful of pause
scrapwood = a pale offcut | a dry bit of alder
strawmat = a handful of bright straw | a twist of dry straw
caketail = the dark sticky corner | the honeyed edge
chalkdot = a dot of chalk | a pale chalk spot
chipbowl = my shallow chip bowl | a saucer for the shavings
strawtip = a clean straw tip | a stiff end of straw
inkspot = a pinprick of ink | a small black blot
carvecut = one shallow cut | a smooth little scoop
paperweight = a tile corner in cloth | a little cloth-covered weight

any
opinion @1 church for 2 : The chapel bench has a dry corner where nobody asks a wolf to give a reason for sitting down.
opinion @1 dole for 3 : I could read the bread notice on an empty belly. Quite another thing to read it chewing.
opinion @1 houses against 2 : A House seal on good thick paper. The explanation for an empty berth comes on a thinner sheet.
opinion @1 watch against 1 : Plenty of ink for warnings to the Sump. Little enough for answers when the Sump writes back.
opinion @1 sump for 2 : There's usually space to put a sore foot up in the Sump, if you ask at the right door.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : A nice copperplate figure doesn't make a dear loaf any heavier.

grown
line @1 greet : Take the dry end of this bench. Welcome. | Come a little out of the wet. There's space.
line @2 greet [here.time=night] : You've brought a shadow with you. Room for both.
line @3 greet [pair.band=strangers] : You're welcome to a quiet minute here.
line @1 greet.return : A decent word travels far enough in a low voice. | I heard you fine from there.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit near. Some things need a familiar ear.
line @1 again.open : I've kept the place where we broke off.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : There's a dry spot saved over here!
line @1 farewell : May the next threshold hold firm for you. | Take your time across the wet stones.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll gather my bits. Away you go.
line @1 react.news : Give me a breathing space to take that in.
line @1 react.news.new : That changes the sense of what I read.
line @1 react.news.known : The words sounded different the first time round.
line @1 react.news.doubt : What mark was underneath it, if any?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|crime|assault|theft] : There's a door somewhere that wants a warm pot left at it.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=work|birth|marriage] : I'd choose handsome big letters for news like that.
line @1 answer.how : A little rasp to my voice. Everything else in order.
line @1 ask.how : Been able to sit somewhere quiet and catch a breath?
line @1 sympathise : You can finish when your mouth will let you.
line @1 reassure : Keep a place at the hard bit. We'll come back and read it together.
line @1 decline : That piece won't bear another airing.
line @1 accept_decline : I'll put the wood away. Your silence will do.
line @1 thank : That's a kindness I can put a finger on.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Leave space for another on the bench sometime.
line @1 preclose : I'm beginning to scrape up shavings with my heel.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go along while the stones are still light.
line @1 ack : I've got the sense of it now.
scene comma
when topic=pastimes self.likes=whittling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : This is #comma#. I cut one end heavy so it sits right. | Hold this. The tail ought to point down.
b : What does it sit on? | Feels like a bent nail. What's it meant to be?
a : My unfinished business, most days. The thing stands on paper and keeps the next sheet from flapping over. | A comma. Good writing ought to allow a wolf a breath.

scene wetstep
when topic=weather self.dislikes=wet_floors
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A patch just inside a doorway goes dark before you slip on it. | I'd leave the first wet print where it shows.
b : Would you mark it somehow? | You'd leave mud on clean boards?
a : A ring of straw. Easy to see, easy to sweep. | Till somebody puts a mat down. Clean boards can cost a wolf a hip.

scene cakecorner
when topic=food self.likes=honey_cakes
does offer / accept / assess
a : Would you take half a honey cake with me? | You can have first pick of the cake.
b : Gladly. You choose the cut. | I'll take the middle if that's fair.
a : Good. Here's the soft half. I'm fond of #caketail#. | Entirely fair. The edge has a bitterness from the baking tin that goes well with the sweet.

scene autumn
when topic=weather self.likes=autumn_wind
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : An autumn gust clears the shavings from a lap nicely. | There's a wind that makes a damp cuff wearable again.
b : Where do those shavings end up? | Out here by the water?
a : Inside my collar the last time. There was a piece missing from my plan. | In a sheltered doorway it will. Face the wrong way and you might as well lick salt.

scene freshfloor
when topic=home self.likes=fresh_straw
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I take the old straw up by rolling it into itself. | I step barefoot on new straw once before I cover it.
b : Does it keep the dust down? | Won't it prick?
a : Some. Then I lay the new straw from beyond the table and leave a clean floor behind me. | One or two scratches. I like to know what's under my feet.

scene smallink
when topic=work self.job=official self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A thumbprint behind a line of type will show through poor paper. | I turn a test sheet and look at the white side first.
b : Does it spoil the message? | What can the blank side tell you?
a : Sometimes all a reader sees is the print behind a word. Then I'd run the notice again. | Whether the ink has come through. A reader deserves one clean face on the sheet.

scene lowbench
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.feeling / sympathise / assess
a : I can manage a damp afternoon better if nobody counts my coughs. | Forgive the breaks in my voice. I'll come to the end presently.
b : I'll hear you when you get there. | There's no race on this bench.
a : That's a better kindness than asking after every little rasp. | Good. I had a whole thought ready and nearly lost it chasing after my breath.

scene halfword
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I don't copy a name from the torn half of a notice. | Somebody left me a tale with the first bit missing.
b : What if you know what belongs there? | How much is missing?
a : Then I know enough to find an unbroken copy. | Enough to turn a warning into an accusation, I think. I'll keep it off my tongue.

scene loanwood
when topic=pastimes self.likes=whittling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : When I get #scrapwood# I look for the split before the shape. | The rough end is the one I ask to keep.
b : Does the split tell you what to carve? | Aren't the ends thrown out for a reason?
a : Yes. Follow it and I have two small pieces. Fight it and I have fuel. | They are. Sometimes that reason is just that the next wolf needs a longer piece.

scene nearhome
when topic=land self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Down Sump way you find the slant under a bench by setting a chip on it. | I keep a short peg for the low foot of a stool.
b : Does a chip roll far enough? | Do all the floors slope here?
a : One little twitch towards the water is enough. I've put hot mugs on worse. | Enough do. I'd sooner carry a peg than hold my tea with both paws.

scene shortbread
when topic=prices self.wealth=poor|destitute
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I pick up a loaf after I hear the price. | You can make a small loaf look a grand size by leaving space round it.
b : To feel the weight? | Does the trick sell bread?
a : Some crusts are grand hollow houses for a spoonful of crumb. | It sells me one good look at the loaf underneath.

scene namecheck
when topic=work self.job=official
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : One printed name can stick to a door long after the paste has gone. | I'd give the smallest type on a public sheet the longest look.
b : Will peeling it off do any good? | Is the small print more likely wrong?
a : Start by reading the name properly before it goes up. Taking it down's the easy part. | No. More likely to go wrong without anyone troubling to look.

scene littlefire
when topic=pastimes self.likes=whittling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I stop carving when a shaving will stand on my thumbnail. | There's a cut you take by feel and a cut that wants light.
b : One that small? | Which cut wants daylight?
a : Yes. If I carry on, the piece gets smaller till it joins the shavings. | Think about keeping the thumb you've got. Doubt is a good reason to wait for morning.

scene savevoice
when topic=day here.time=dawn
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My first word in the morning often comes out as a whisper. | I have a swallow of warm water before I answer the day.
b : Does it get stronger? | What does the day ask of you?
a : Generally. No need to start every morning proving it. | Usually where I put my other shoe. A quiet question deserves a quiet answer.

scene signedsheet
when topic=news
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I worry over notices that have lost their bottom edge. | I've seen a scrap go a long way on the authority of a torn corner.
b : Why that particular edge? | What would settle it for you?
a : It's where the name often sits. Somebody ought to own an order. | A whole sheet and a legible mark. Then we could speak about the order itself.

scene byfriend
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I save the near end of the bench for #self.friend#. | If #self.friend# comes by, there's a half-cake saved.
b : Do you talk much on that bench? | Won't it go hard?
a : Enough to tell when there's no more need. | Maybe. That's why there's tea.

scene drydrawer
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I've designed myself a drawer that never sticks in wet weather. | In my sleep my best wood grows back when I carve too deep.
b : How does the drawer do that? | Would you ever finish a piece?
a : Haven't reached that part of the dream. So far it just smells wonderful inside. | I think I'd spend most of my time putting the wood back.

scene lightend
when topic=day here.time=night|dusk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I trim the rough little knobs by daylight and polish by lamplight. | When the street turns grey I can still feel a sharp edge.
b : Why save the polishing? | Is that enough to carve by?
a : A fingertip can find the last rough patch. My eyes deserve an evening off. | Enough to stop. I'll rub a finished piece smooth until the edge shows bright again.

story emptyline [topic=memories self.job=official]
  : Once I set a short notice so tightly the words ran into one another.
  : I took it out to read from across the way. It looked like a single black bar.
  b : Could you spare another sheet to print it again?
  : I found one. The blank strip I left down the middle did more good than a dozen fresh words.

story leafpile [topic=pastimes self.likes=whittling]
  : I carved a leaf, thin enough to let the hearth shine through.
  : Set it down to admire the grain. A gust carried it into my bowl of shavings.
  b : How did you pick it out?
  : I didn't. That evening the bowl lit a fire and I had a completely different sort of light through my leaf.

story honeywrap [topic=food self.likes=honey_cakes]
  : A honey cake once stuck fast to the cloth I'd saved to carry it home.
  : I tried to peel it off while it was cold. Got a fair mouthful of thread.
  b : How did you eat the rest?
  : I hung the cloth by the kettle, let it warm, and gave the cake a push. Down it came, taking every fold like an elderly caller on the stairs.

rule @damp
when concept=bark self.dislikes=wet_floors
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Mind that shine inside the door. | Put straw across the slick patch first.

rule @press
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=official
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Give the damp sheet room to lie flat. | Clear print on the front, clean paper behind!

rule @chips
when concept=bark self.likes=whittling
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Mind my chips, there's a sharp one. | Let that curl fall clear.

rule @finish
when concept=bark here.time=dusk|night
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Light's gone off the cutting edge. | Knives down, rubbing cloth out.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I'll hear a caller from this dry bit of floor. Stand where you're comfortable.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : A place beside me again, #topic.player#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : We can stay civil across the width of a bench.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=dawn] : A quiet word will carry this early.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=official] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Plenty goes into giving a word its proper space.
dlg @2 resident.job : A careful paw where a small thing wants putting right.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : A piece worth reading all the way through. #world.news.capitalize#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Leave your wet footprints going safely home.
graft resident.hub
choice May I hold the little carved comma? -> rests_in_hand
node rests_in_hand
say Cup your paw so the tail runs into the crease. You can feel the place where my knife caught. It fits me better for the nick.
choice Why a comma of all things? -> worth_of_space
choice Put it back on the page. -> hub
node worth_of_space
say Because you can stop there without coming to an end. It's a fine courtesy to wolves with more words than breath.
choice Leave a moment of quiet. -> hub
choice Say goodbye. -> bye
